=== Annual Events ===
; Main Festival (September 16)
: Centered on the festival held on September 16, various rituals including mikoshi processions take place from September 11 to 21. Because these events continue “dara dara” (i.e. dragging on) over a long period, the festival has long been known as the “Dara Dara Festival.” In addition, because ginger is distributed during this period, it is also called the “Ginger Festival” (see the section “Offerings” below).
; Savings Festival (October 17)
: In commemoration of the achievements of {{ill|Makino Motonanjirō|ja|牧野元次郎|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Makino Motonanjirō|qq=}} (1874–1942), who established a savings bank (the predecessor of the present {{ill|Resona Bank|ja|りそな銀行|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Resona Bank|qq=}}, formerly Asahi Bank and Kyōwa Bank) in the vicinity, a “Savings Mound” (with an inscription by {{ill|Musha Kojii Atsutoku|ja|武者小路実篤|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Musha Kojii Atsutoku|qq=}}) was erected on the shrine grounds in Shōwa 32 (1957), and ever since, annual prayers for the protection of savings have been conducted.
; Setsubun Festival (February)
: On this day the “Hanchō Festival” is held. This ritual is performed by the “Megumi” fire brigade of Edo town—which administers the shrine’s parish area. It is said that during an incident known as the “Megumi Fight” that took place on the shrine grounds in February of Bunka 2 (1805), the ringing of the hanchō (half bell) led to a great commotion. (The hanchō was once exiled but was later returned to the shrine in the Meiji period.)

