After 1876, due to mergers and fires (for example, during the Great Kantō Earthquake), in Shōwa 11 ({{ill|1936|ja|1936|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1936|qq=}}) on September 12 a single subsidiary shrine was rebuilt; with the merger of all the auxiliary shrines, since deities such as {{ill|Ōkuninushi|ja|大国主命|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ōkuninushi|qq=}}, {{ill|Sukunahikona|ja|少彦名命|1=Sukunabikona|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sukunahikona|13=User:Immanuelle/Sukunabikona|qq=}}, and {{ill|Kotoshironushi-no-Mikoto|lt=Kotoshironushi-no-Mikoto|lt=Kotoshironushi|ja|事代主命|qq=draft|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kotoshironushi|qq=}} were enshrined, the shrine came to be regarded as one that confers protection for milestones (such as 60th, 70th, 77th, or 99th birthdays). However, these too were destroyed during the conflagration of the Second World War.<ref group="Notes">For reference, below is the situation of the merged shrines in Shōwa 11 (listing the old auxiliary shrine names, principal deities, and their divine virtues):
